### What the `# tree:` headers honestly name
All 11 read `# tree: dirty:19fb55c3c974da0d851e0ebd202d5ad09082ade3`.
hashRepo (lifecycle.ts:515) = sourceTreeHash(repo, excludes, "HEAD") over
`git ls-tree -r HEAD` + an isDirtyOutside flag. Recomputed with the same
function and excludes [studio, .bisellium, examples/]:
  babba83 19fb55c3c974da0d851e0ebd202d5ad09082ade3
  ec553fa 19fb55c3c974da0d851e0ebd202d5ad09082ade3
  b67066f 1a48d5a4bec3a50e94ece16fc9f28bf8096ea40e
  1091a08 1a48d5a4bec3a50e94ece16fc9f28bf8096ea40e
So the header truthfully says "committed source = fork point, working tree had
uncommitted non-excluded edits". It does NOT fingerprint the tested content:
the test file and a (hypothetically) edited lifecycle.ts would produce the same
header. That is why log 10 (23:33Z) and 01-04/09/11 (23:41Z) share one hash.
Old-source provenance of the reds is established by the reproduction above,
not by the header. Class note (pre-existing, not W-034's): 161 of 172 reds in
studio/ci/reds carry `dirty:` (W-020..W-044), inherent to test-first recording
on an uncommitted test. Candidate retro lesson; no W-034 action.

